<title>ITALY: Pirelli cons net profit up 43 pct in 1996.</title>
<headline>Pirelli cons net profit up 43 pct in 1996.</headline>
<dateline>MILAN 1997-03-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year ending December 31, 1996</p>
<p>(In billion lire unless stated)</p>
<p>    Cons net profit		   436	 vs    304</p>
<p>    Net income after minorities   387	 vs    257</p>
<p>    Ord dividend			100 lire  vs     50 lire</p>
<p>    Net sales			10,240	 (down six pct)</p>
<p>    Cons operating profit	   680	 vs    636</p>
<p>    Gross operating margin	1,208	 vs  1,186</p>
<p>Extraordinary income/</p>
<p>			expenses	 (138)	vs   (108)</p>
<p>    Shareholders equity	   3,714	 vs  3,784</p>
<p>    Net debt			  1,015	 vs  1,406</p>
<p>    Parent net result		 261.4     vs    140.8</p>
<p>    Operating result		  222.3     vs    106.2</p>
<p>    Extraordinary income	     45.3     vs     41.3</p>
<p>    Shareholders equity	   2,796.8     vs  2,636.1</p>
<p>    Net financial position	3,384.2     vs  3,047.6</p>
<p>NOTE - Pirelli said in a statement the outlook for 1997 looked fragmented &quot;and not homogeneous&quot; geographically, with some sectors in which the group operates showing &quot;a strong increase in competition especially on the price side.&quot;</p>
<p>The trend in raw materials prices would also play an important role after steady price falls in the last two years.</p>
<p>However the group would continue investment policy focused on product innovation and new technology and it said the expected market trend and measures already taken meant it forecast a positive trend for the group in 1997.</p>
<p>--Milan bureau +392 66129507</p>
</text>
